AB  - Shelf life determinations under non-refrigerated conditions, especially high temperature regimes characteristic of tropical/subtropical regions, deserve more attention. In this study, we investigated effects of modified atmosphere packaging (MAP) on longevity of conidia of Beauveria bassiana (Bb) strain GHA. Similar rates of conidial survival were observed after storage for 60days at 50AC in atmospheres of pure COa, Na, Ha, or He (49a51% viability), but few conidia (a122%) survived storage in Oa-rich atmospheres. Viability of conidia stored in an atmosphere of 20% COa/80% Na decreased to 80% within 180days at 40AC and within 30days at 50AC but remained high (87%) after a 16-month storage period at 25AC. Oa concentrations in the storage containers ranged from 0.3% at the start to as high as 12.4% at the end of experiments (due to container leakage). When active packaging (hermetically sealed packages with Oa/moisture scavengers) was employed, shelf lives were substantially improved. Viabilities a3480% were consistently recorded after 6months at 40AC or 2months at 50AC when a dual Oa/moisture absorber or a combination of sachets (dual Oa absorber/COa generator+desiccant) were used. Water activities (aw) supporting greatest survival were a120.030, suggesting that optimal aw for long-term storage under anaerobic conditions is lower than determined in previous studies of storage in the presence of Oa. Additionally, we have shown that actively packa
