AB  - The effects of various spray-application parameters on the efficacy of Beauveria bassiana foliar treatments against Leptinotarsa decemlineata larvae were evaluated during three field seasons. Treatments were applied to small plots (6 rows x 7-12 m) using a CO2-powered backpack hydraulic sprayer with nozzles affixed to lateral drop tubes and directed upward at a 45 degrees angle to target ventral leaf surfaces. The sprayer delivered 280 liters/ha at 3.45 bar. Three rates (1.25, 2.5, and 5 x 10(13) conidia/ha) of an emulsifiable oil formulation of B. bassiana and two spray intervals (3-4 vs 6-8 days) were tested. Three applications at the medium and high rates made at 3- to 4-day intervals produced low to moderate levels of control (43-65%). The low rate and applications at weekly intervals were less effective. A wettable powder (WP) formulation was also compared to the oil formulation. Three applications at the medium rate were made at 3- to 4-day intervals. During one field season, rain commenced soon after two of the three initial applications. Under these conditions, the oil formulation was significantly more effective than the WP formulation (65% vs 8% control), suggesting greater rainfastness of the oil formulation. Tests with the oil formulation also compared applications from a sprayer configured with drop tubes as described above to those from a sprayer configured with nozzles directed to spray downward from 30 cm above the canopy. The sprayer with nozzles attached sol
