AB  - Successful infection of the insect body by entomopathogenic fungi is the result of complex molecular interactions between the host and the invading pathogenic fungi. The mosquito antifungal response is multifaceted and is regulated in part by the Toll and Jak-STAT pathways. Here, we assessed the role of the IMD pathway in the mosquito Ae. aegypti antifungal immune response when challenged with one of two entomopathogenic fungi, Beauveria bassiana and Isaria javanica. IMD pathway components of the mosquito immune system were elicited in response to infection with both entomopathogenic fungi, primarily in the fat body of mosquitoes. Furthermore, we observed induction of antimicrobial peptides that in turn appear to be tissue and fungal strain-specific. IMD pathway impairment by RNAi gene silencing resulted in higher fungal proliferation and reduction in survival of fungi-infected mosquitoes. Collectively, these data indicates that the IMD pathway plays a more significant role in the antifungal immune response than previously recognized.
